  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 256 AM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nAn unusual upper-level flow pattern is expected late Sunday as \na trough exits the central CONUS. Ensemble guidance projects \nmeridional flow aloft for the County Warning Area (CWA) as a \nridge slides in from the northern Rockies. Most model runs are \nnow anticipating this building ridge to propagate faster to the \neast than prior runs. Simultaneously, the trough seems to slow \ndown a bit as it makes its way eastward across the southern \nplains. Northeast upper-level winds should result as the exiting\ntrough becomes more positively tilted than originally thought. \nMoving on to Monday, said ridge finally pushes the resisting \ntrough away and the system is able to proceed with it's eastward\npropagation across our region. Initially, northerly flow aloft \nis expected Monday transitioning briefly to more of a zonal look\nearly Tuesday as the ridge axis passes over the CWA. Weak \nsouthwest flow is then forecasted later in the day Tuesday, with\nzonal flow once again taking over shortly after. Guidance is \nnow starting to lock onto a stronger ridge system beginning to \ninfluence our coverage area on Wednesday, ushering in northwest \nwinds at the 300-500mb level.\n\nOne may wonder what is to come from this peculiar synoptic \nsetup across the Tri-State area. The answer is likely nothing \nbut dry conditions and seasonably pleasant temperatures as high \npressure is set to take control both higher up in the atmosphere\nand at the surface. Furthermore, moisture content will be quite\nlimited as dewpoints in the 30s/40s and Relative Humidity (RH) \nvalues ranging 25-55% are forecast. Surface analysis does \nanticipate three frontal boundaries to move through during this \nforecast period (one each on Sunday, Tuesday and Wednesday) but \ndry air should limit any chance for precipitation. Therefore, \nrain will be highly unlikely as Probability of Precipitation \nfall below 5% for the entire long-term forecast period.\n\nHigh temperatures, as mentioned above, should be quite pleasant\nfor this time of the year across the CWA. Daily highs in the \n60s will be commonplace Sunday through the middle of next week. \nOvernight lows should follow a slight warming trend. Low \ntemperatures at or above freezing on Sunday morning likely \nincreases to upper 30s/low 40s Monday morning through Wednesday \nmorning. Some locales may not even see morning temps fall below \n42 degrees on Wednesday. It is not likely to be very windy at \nall going into next week as guidance outlines general 5-15 mph \nwinds Sunday through Wednesday with some windier prone areas of \neastern Colorado potentially seeing up to 20 mph sustained \nwinds. Sunday likely is the windiest day but gusts shouldn't \nexceed 25 mph. RH values may dip into the low 20s Sunday/Monday \nfor areas at or west of US-385 and 20-30% for everywhere in the \nCWA east of there but dry fuels are not anticipated so fire \nweather chances will be low. Sunday will be the only day to \nmonitor for that but again, there's a very low chance of even \nelevated fire weather occurring in eastern Colorado.",
